WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.640
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:08.080
Today we are going to have a look at this insane&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:13.120
light transport simulation paper and&nbsp;
get our minds blown in three chapters.

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:20.000
Chapter 1. Radiosity. Radiosity is an&nbsp;
old old light transport algorithm that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:26.800
can simulate the flow of light within a scene.&nbsp;
And it can generate a scene of this quality.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:33.840
Well, not the best, clearly, but this&nbsp;
technique is from decades ago. Back in the day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:39.200
this was a simple and intuitive attempt&nbsp;
at creating a light simulation program,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:45.840
and quite frankly, the best we could do. It&nbsp;
goes something like this: slice up the scene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:51.840
into tiny surfaces, and compute the light&nbsp;
transport between these tiny surfaces.

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:58.240
This worked reasonably well for diffuse&nbsp;
light transport, matte objects if you will.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:06.480
But, it was not great at rendering shiny objects.&nbsp;
And it gets even worse, look. You see these blocky&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:12.320
artifacts here, these come from the fact that the&nbsp;
scene has been subdivided into these surfaces,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:17.680
and the surfaces are not fine enough&nbsp;
for these boundaries to disappear.

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:21.920
But, yes, I hear you asking, Károly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:29.280
why talk about this ancient technique? I’ll tell&nbsp;
you in a moment. You’ll see, I promise. So, yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:35.520
radiosity is old, some professors still teach&nbsp;
it to their students, it makes an interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:42.000
history lesson, but I haven’t seen any use of&nbsp;
this technique in the industry in decades now.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:48.960
If radiosity would be a vehicle, it would be a&nbsp;
horse carriage in the age of high-tech Tesla cars.

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:54.320
So, I know what you’re thinking. Yes,&nbsp;
let’s have a look at those Teslas.

00:01:54.880 --> 00:02:00.000
Chapter 2. Neural rendering. Many&nbsp;
modern light simulation programs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:06.640
can now simulate proper light transport with&nbsp;
shiny objects, none of these blocky artifacts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:12.880
they are, as you see, absolutely amazing.&nbsp;
They can render all kinds of material models,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:19.520
detailed geometry, caustics, color bleeding,&nbsp;
you name it. However, they seem to start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:26.640
out from a noisy image, and as we compute the&nbsp;
path of more and more light rays, this noisy image&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:35.840
clears up over time. But, this still takes a&nbsp;
while. How long? Well, from minutes to days.

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:43.600
And then, neural rendering entered the&nbsp;
fray. Here you see our earlier paper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:48.560
that replaced the whole light simulation&nbsp;
program with a neural network that learned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:55.440
how to do this. And it can create these images&nbsp;
so quickly that it easily runs not in minutes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.440 --> 00:03:03.680
or days, but as fast as you see here. Yes,&nbsp;
in real time on a commodity graphics card.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:07.760
Now note that this neural renderer&nbsp;
is limited to this particular scene.

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:12.560
With this, I hope that it is&nbsp;
easy to see that we are now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:20.320
so far beyond radiosity that it sounds like a&nbsp;
distant memory of the olden times. So, once again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:25.680
why talk about radiosity? Well, check this out.

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:33.120
Chapter 3. Neural Radiosity. Excuse&nbsp;
me, what? Yes, you heard it right.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:40.480
This paper is about neural radiosity. This&nbsp;
work elevates the old old radiosity algorithm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:47.920
by using a similar formulation to the original&nbsp;
technique, but also, infusing it with a powerful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.920 --> 00:03:54.320
neural network. It is using the same horse&nbsp;
carriage, but strapping it onto a rocket,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.320 --> 00:04:00.160
if you will. Now you have my attention.&nbsp;
So, let’s see what this can do together.

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:08.320
Look. Hmm! Yes, it can render really&nbsp;
intense specular highlights. And now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:16.160
hold on to your papers, and wow. Once again,&nbsp;
the results look like a nearly pixel-perfect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:23.120
copy of the reference simulation. And, we now&nbsp;
understand the limitations of the old radiosity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:30.720
so, let’s strike where it hurts the most.&nbsp;
Yes, perfectly specular, mirror-like surfaces.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:37.600
Let’s see what happens here. Well, I can&nbsp;
hardly believe what I am seeing here!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:44.800
No issues whatsoever. Still, close&nbsp;
to pixel perfect. This new paper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:51.200
truly elevates the good old radiosity&nbsp;
to the next level. So good! Loving it.

00:04:51.920 --> 00:04:58.880
But wait a second. I hear you asking, yes,&nbsp;
Károly, this is all well and good, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:04.720
if we have the reference simulation, why&nbsp;
not just use that? Good question. Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:11.360
that's right, the reference is great, but that&nbsp;
one takes up to several hours to compute, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.360 --> 00:05:19.120
the new technique can be done super quickly. Yet,&nbsp;
they look almost exactly the same. My goodness.

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:26.560
In fact, let’s look at an equal-time comparison&nbsp;
against one of the Tesla techniques, path tracing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:33.520
We give the two techniques the same amount of&nbsp;
time, and see what they can produce. Let’s see.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.640 --> 00:05:42.640
Now that is no contest. Look. This is Eric Veach’s&nbsp;
legendary scene where the light only comes from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.640 --> 00:05:49.920
the neighboring room through a door that is only&nbsp;
slightly ajar. Notoriously difficult for any kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:55.760
of light transport algorithm. And yet, look&nbsp;
at how good this new one is in tackling it.

00:05:56.800 --> 00:06:03.920
Now, note that not even this technique is perfect.&nbsp;
It has two caveats. One, the training takes place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.920 --> 00:06:09.680
per-scene. This means that we need to give&nbsp;
these scenes to the neural network in advance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.680 --> 00:06:16.640
so it can learn how light bounces off of this&nbsp;
place, and this can take from minutes to hours.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:23.680
But, for instance, if we have a video game&nbsp;
with a limited set of places that you can go,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.680 --> 00:06:29.760
we can train our neural network on all of&nbsp;
them in advance, and deploy it to the players,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:37.040
who can then enjoy it for as long as they wish.&nbsp;
No more training is required after that. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.040 --> 00:06:41.440
the technique would still need to be&nbsp;
a little faster than it currently is.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.160 --> 00:06:46.640
And two, we also need quite a bit&nbsp;
of memory to perform all this.

00:06:47.200 --> 00:06:53.440
And, yes! I think this is an excellent&nbsp;
place to invoke the First Law Of Papers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.440 --> 00:06:58.160
which says that research is a&nbsp;
process. Do not look at where we are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.160 --> 00:07:04.080
look at where we will be two more papers down&nbsp;
the line. And two more papers down the line,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:10.480
who knows, maybe we get this in real time and&nbsp;
with a much friendlier memory consumption.

00:07:11.120 --> 00:07:17.040
Now, there is one more area that I think would&nbsp;
be an excellent direction for future work. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.040 --> 00:07:21.440
that is about the per-scene training&nbsp;
of the neural network. In this work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.440 --> 00:07:28.080
it has to get a feel of the scene before the light&nbsp;
simulation happens. So how does the knowledge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:34.720
learned on one scene transfer to others? I imagine&nbsp;
that it should be possible to create a more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:40.560
general version of this that does not need to look&nbsp;
at a new scene before the simulation takes place.

00:07:41.200 --> 00:07:48.240
And in summary. I absolutely love this&nbsp;
paper. I takes an old old algorithm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:52.640
blows the dust off of it, and&nbsp;
completely reinvigorates it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.640 --> 00:07:57.840
by infusing it with a modern learning-based&nbsp;
technique. What a time to be alive!

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:03.920
So, what do you think? What would you use&nbsp;
this for? I’d love to hear your thoughts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.920 --> 00:08:06.240
please let me know in the comments below.

